 "reading": "No measured within-block quantity tracks the 16-tag level. The three equal-size blocks differ in fitted offset by at most **0.0385** (**0.4200** / **0.4065** / **0.3815**), which is **0.39** to **1.68** standard errors of the pairwise difference at six seeds each and no wider than the within-block seed spread (**0.043** / **0.073** / **0.036**). Their candidate-space quantities barely move: mean support **22.90** / **22.61** / **22.62**, support mass **1458** / **1443** / **1444**, support dispersion **7.88** / **7.77** / **8.14**, tag entropy **3.9060** / **3.9021** / **3.8955**, pair saturation **0.442** / **0.443** / **0.443**, and **9** cell pairs in all three. Three of eight quantities order the three block means the way the level does \u2014 median support (**22.33** / **22.00** / **21.50**), tag entropy and the share at support <= 10 (**0.045** / **0.034** / **0.029**) \u2014 which is what a chance ordering returns about one time in six, and none keeps a consistent sign at seed resolution: median support runs **-0.62** / **+0.38** / **-0.09**, tag entropy **-0.77** / **+0.14** / **-0.46**, and the floor share reverses the block ordering outright, **+0.14** / **-0.60** / **-0.82**. The sharpest single case against a support account is fresh-16's lowest seed, `20650105` at offset **+0.2589**, which has that block's **highest** mean support (**23.0**). The lowest block matches fresh-16 on every quantity measured, including candidate count (**63.8** against **63.8**) and K completeness (**1.000**), so the level is not carried by candidate support, support mass, support dispersion, entropy, cell-pair coverage or pair saturation: it varies more inside a block than between equal-size blocks, and the fitted block offset is a mean of draws rather than a property of the candidate space.",
